soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: the message box clock, a link to the watchdog, the window the DT declares

Three things this driver relied on without saying so.

The message box's bus clock belonged to cpu_comm alone; every exchange
with the ARISC worked because cpu_comm, U-Boot or clk_ignore_unused kept
it on. It is now taken here too, optional, so a DT without it still
binds. The reset is not: cpu_comm requests it exclusive and only ever
releases it, nothing in Linux asserts it, and a second requester would
only make one of the two drivers fail to probe.

The SoC watchdog has to stop in its suspend_noirq and start again in its
resume_noirq, and crust's sleep net is armed in this driver's
suspend_noirq. Which of the two runs first followed the dpm list, which
a deferred probe reorders. "allwinner,watchdog" names the watchdog, and
a stateless device link makes this driver its consumer: its noirq runs
after ours on the way down and before ours on the way up. Without the
property there is no link, and a crust built with the net gets no
standby (refused where the standby checks are).

The SRAM window was widened at runtime to reach crust's contract page
when the DT declared only the vendor image's 0x23000 bytes. The DT
declares the page now, and this driver maps nothing the DT does not
describe. So the DT change of the arisc node (the window up to 0x123c00)
has to come before this one, and DT and module go out together. Should a
DT with the short window meet this driver anyway - say one that already
names crust in firmware-name, or a crust started by TF-A with no file to
keep it by - crust can be neither loaded nor kept, and the driver loads
the vendor image instead, with an error that names the window: HDMI
comes up, standby does not, rather than the whole probe failing.

